{
  "image_name": "03d50a3dc842f8867a474e1b5b31921b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how monocytes enter the vessel wall and develop into different macrophage states during atherosclerosis. Circulating monocytes cross the endothelium from the lumen into the lesion, where transcription factors such as PU.1, C/EBPβ, and AP-1 drive their differentiation into macrophages. Within the plaque, these cells adopt several phenotypes, including pro-inflammatory, anti-inflammatory, and a macrophage-like smooth muscle cell state.\n\nThe local environment appears to shape these identities. Lipids and signals linked to PPARγ and LXR promote one macrophage program, while IL-4 and IL-10 are associated with another, involving factors like STAT6, MYC, PPARγ, and IRF4. In contrast, LDL, oxidized LDL, and cholesterol crystals favor inflammatory activation through pathways including NF-κB, IRF1, IRF5, AP-1, and Ikaros. Vascular smooth muscle cells also seem able to contribute to macrophage-like cells through KLF4.\n\nA central outcome is foam cell formation, followed by apoptotic foam cells and buildup of a necrotic core within the atherosclerotic lesion. ATF3 and MAFB are shown as inhibitory regulators that restrain progression toward foam cells and apoptotic foam cells, highlighting checkpoints in plaque development.",
